<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Micaforge profile override for ClickHouse.

  Mounted at /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/micaforge.xml. It tunes the `default`
  profile, which is the profile every Micaforge query runs under.

  It deliberately defines no users. The account itself is created by the image's
  entrypoint from CLICKHOUSE_USER / CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD / CLICKHOUSE_DB, which it
  writes into users.d/default-user.xml alongside this file. Two files in the same
  directory, touching different parts of the tree, merge cleanly; a second copy of
  the user definition here would be one more place for the password to drift out
  of step with .env.

  Same audience as config.xml: one 4 GB VPS. config.xml bounds what the *server*
  may use. This file bounds what a single *query* may use, which is the limit that
  turns "the box died" into "that one query returned an error".
-->
<clickhouse>
  <profiles>
    <default>

      <!--
        Per-query ceiling, and per-user ceiling across concurrent queries. Both
        sit under the server ceiling in config.xml, so one expensive breakdown
        over a year of events fails on its own rather than starving the ingest
        path that is writing at the same time.

        A query that hits this is usually an unbounded GROUP BY over a high
        cardinality dimension. The two external-memory settings below are the
        reason it will normally spill to disk instead of failing.
      -->
      <max_memory_usage>1000000000</max_memory_usage>
      <max_memory_usage_for_user>1200000000</max_memory_usage_for_user>

      <!--
        Spill to disk at half the query budget. Slower than memory, and much
        faster than an error. This is what makes a full-history export work on a
        machine that cannot hold the intermediate state.
      -->
      <max_bytes_before_external_group_by>500000000</max_bytes_before_external_group_by>
      <max_bytes_before_external_sort>500000000</max_bytes_before_external_sort>

      <!--
        Nothing a dashboard renders is worth a minute of CPU on a shared box, and
        the API's own timeout is shorter than this. A query that runs into this
        limit is a bug or a filter nobody meant to remove; either way an error is
        the useful outcome.
      -->
      <max_execution_time>60</max_execution_time>

      <!--
        Two vCPUs is the assumption. ClickHouse defaults max_threads to the core
        count, which is right for a dedicated server and wrong here: four threads
        per query keeps a single dashboard load from taking the whole machine
        away from ingest.
      -->
      <max_threads>4</max_threads>

      <!--
        The uncompressed cache pays off for repeated point reads of the same
        rows. Analytics scans do not repeat that way, so it would be memory spent
        on nothing. Off is also the ClickHouse default; it is stated here because
        it is a decision, not an oversight.
      -->
      <use_uncompressed_cache>0</use_uncompressed_cache>

      <!--
        Asynchronous inserts are off on purpose. The Rust server already batches
        events in memory and flushes on size or interval (see
        apps/backend/crates/store/src/writer.rs). Turning them on here would add a
        second, invisible buffer with its own durability window, and make "where
        is my event" a two-place question instead of one.
      -->
      <async_insert>0</async_insert>

      <!--
        Keep query_log rows for the queries Micaforge runs. config.xml gives them
        a 7 day TTL, so this costs a bounded amount of disk and is the only way to
        answer "which dashboard screen is slow" after the fact.
      -->
      <log_queries>1</log_queries>

    </default>
  </profiles>

  <!--
    Quotas are left at the distribution default, which is unlimited.

    A quota limits queries per interval per user, and there is exactly one user
    here: the Micaforge server. Rate limiting it would throttle the dashboard
    without protecting anything, because the limits that matter on this box are
    the memory ceilings above. Add a quota if you open ClickHouse to something
    other than Micaforge, which the shipped compose file does not do.
  -->
</clickhouse>
